| sort - events
| fields vendor, events, pct, senders, sample_senders, first_seen, last_seen



# Initial Access Hunting — Default Windows Auditing + Deny-Only Firewall

## What this telemetry can and cannot answer

**Cannot:** process execution, command lines, script content, DLL loads, file writes,
registry changes, successful network connections, C2 beaconing over allowed ports.

**Can:** authentication patterns, credential use, service and task creation, pre-attack
scanning, blocked lateral movement, blocked egress.

**The core limitation.** A deny-only firewall log records traffic that did *not* reach
its destination. Successful initial access is allowed traffic and will never appear in
it. Every firewall query below is therefore inferential: it finds the reconnaissance
that preceded access, or the failed movement that followed it, and uses timing to
bracket the event you cannot see directly.

Authentication is the primary lens. Everything else is corroboration.

---

## Part 1 — Regex extraction

Assumes Windows events arriving as syslog text rather than structured. If you have the
Splunk TA for Windows bound correctly, these fields already exist and you can skip to
Part 2.

### 4624 / 4625 core fields

```spl
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Logon Type:\s+(?<LogonType>\d+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)New Logon:.*?Account Name:\s+(?<TargetUser>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)New Logon:.*?Account Domain:\s+(?<TargetDomain>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Source Network Address:\s+(?<SrcIp>[0-9a-fA-F\.:]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Source Port:\s+(?<SrcPort>\d+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Workstation Name:\s+(?<Workstation>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Logon Process:\s+(?<LogonProcess>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Authentication Package:\s+(?<AuthPackage>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Process Name:\s+(?<CallerProcess>[^\r\n]+)"
```

The `(?s)` flag makes `.` match newlines, which is required because these are multi-line
events. Without it the lookahead patterns fail silently.

For 4625 specifically, the substatus is the field that carries the intent:

```spl
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Failure Information:.*?Status:\s+(?<Status>0x[0-9A-Fa-f]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Sub Status:\s+(?<SubStatus>0x[0-9A-Fa-f]+)"
| eval failure_reason=case(
    SubStatus=="0xC0000064", "user does not exist",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006A", "wrong password",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000234", "account locked out",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000072", "account disabled",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000070", "workstation restriction",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000193", "account expired",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000071", "password expired",
    SubStatus=="0xC000015B", "logon type not granted",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006D", "generic bad credentials",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006E", "account restriction",
    1=1, SubStatus)
```

**This distinction does most of the work.** `0xC0000064` across many accounts is
*enumeration* — the attacker is discovering valid usernames. `0xC000006A` across many
accounts is *spraying* — they already have the list and are guessing passwords. Those
are different stages and they call for different responses.

### Logon type decode

```spl
| eval logon_desc=case(
    LogonType=="2",  "Interactive (console)",
    LogonType=="3",  "Network (SMB/share)",
    LogonType=="4",  "Batch (scheduled task)",
    LogonType=="5",  "Service",
    LogonType=="7",  "Unlock",
    LogonType=="8",  "NetworkCleartext",
    LogonType=="9",  "NewCredentials (runas /netonly)",
    LogonType=="10", "RemoteInteractive (RDP)",
    LogonType=="11", "CachedInteractive",
    1=1, "unknown")
```

Types 3, 8, 9, and 10 are where initial access and lateral movement live. Type 8 is
notable on its own — cleartext credentials over the network, typical of basic-auth
web apps and some legacy tooling, and worth knowing about regardless of attack.

### 4648 — explicit credentials

The single most underused default event. Fires when a process authenticates as an
account other than the one it is running under.

```spl
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Account Whose Credentials Were Used:.*?Account Name:\s+(?<UsedAccount>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Target Server:.*?Target Server Name:\s+(?<TargetServer>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Process Information:.*?Process Name:\s+(?<CallerProcess>[^\r\n]+)"
```

### System log 7045 — service install

Not in the Security log and not dependent on any audit policy. Always present.

```spl
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Service Name:\s+(?<ServiceName>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Service File Name:\s+(?<ServiceFile>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Service Type:\s+(?<ServiceType>[^\r\n]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Service Start Type:\s+(?<StartType>[^\r\n]+)"
```

### Firewall deny

Normalize before analysis; field names differ per vendor.

```spl
| eval src_ip=coalesce(src_ip, src, 'id.orig_h')
| eval dest_ip=coalesce(dest_ip, dest, 'id.resp_h')
| eval dest_port=coalesce(dest_port, dport, 'id.resp_p')
```

---

## Part 2 — Trend analytics

Regex gives you fields. These give you findings.

### H1 — Password spray

Many accounts, few attempts each, one source, short window. Inverts the per-account
lockout threshold, which is exactly why it evades default alerting.

```spl
<4625 base search + extractions>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(TargetUser) AS users_tried, count AS attempts, values(failure_reason) AS reasons BY _time, SrcIp
| eval attempts_per_user=round(attempts/users_tried,2)
| where users_tried >= 10 AND attempts_per_user <= 3
| sort - users_tried
```

**Then check whether it worked** — the step most spray hunts skip:

```spl
<4624 base search> 
| search SrcIp IN (<spray source from above>)
| stats min(_time) AS first_success, values(TargetUser) AS compromised BY SrcIp
```

A 4625 storm followed by a 4624 from the same source is the finding. The storm alone is
just noise.

### H2 — Account enumeration

```spl
<4625 base search + extractions>
| where SubStatus="0xC0000064"
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(TargetUser) AS nonexistent_accounts, values(TargetUser) AS sample BY _time, SrcIp, Workstation
| where nonexistent_accounts >= 15
```

`Workstation` is worth keeping — attacker tooling often sets a static or obviously
synthetic workstation name, and that becomes a durable pivot.

### H3 — First-ever source for an account

Rarity beats threshold for initial access. This finds an account authenticating from
somewhere it never has.

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType IN ("3","8","10")
| stats min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen, count BY TargetUser, SrcIp
| eventstats min(first_seen) AS account_first_ever BY TargetUser
| where first_seen > relative_time(now(), "-7d") AND first_seen != account_first_ever
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| sort first_seen
```

Run once over 90 days to build intuition for your baseline before scheduling it.

### H4 — Impossible / anomalous RDP

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType="10"
| where NOT (cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", SrcIp) OR cidrmatch("172.16.0.0/12", SrcIp) OR cidrmatch("192.168.0.0/16", SrcIp))
| stats count, values(TargetUser) AS users, dc(host) AS hosts_reached BY SrcIp
| sort - count
```

External RDP success is a finding on its own merits. Corroborate with the Terminal
Services LocalSessionManager operational log (events 21, 25) and RemoteConnectionManager
1149, both on by default.

### H5 — Off-hours authentication

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType IN ("3","10")
| eval hour=strftime(_time,"%H"), dow=strftime(_time,"%w")
| where (hour < 6 OR hour > 20) OR dow IN ("0","6")
| stats count, values(SrcIp) AS sources, values(host) AS targets BY TargetUser
| where count >= 3
```

Noisy in shops with real shift work or overseas teams. Baseline per account rather than
globally if that describes you.

### H6 — Service install as persistence

```spl
<7045 base search + extractions>
| eval suspicious=case(
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)\\\\(Temp|Users\\\\Public|ProgramData|AppData|Windows\\\\Temp)\\\\"), "path",
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)(powershell|cmd\.exe|wscript|cscript|rundll32|mshta|regsvr32)"), "interpreter",
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)-enc|-e\s|frombase64|IEX|DownloadString"), "encoded",
    match(ServiceName, "^[a-zA-Z0-9]{8,16}$") AND NOT match(ServiceName, "(?i)(update|sync|agent|monitor)"), "random name",
    1=1, null())
| where isnotnull(suspicious)
| table _time, host, ServiceName, ServiceFile, StartType, suspicious
```

PsExec, Impacket `smbexec`, and Cobalt Strike's service jump all surface here. This is
your single best execution-adjacent detection without 4688.

### H7 — Firewall: scanning preceding a logon

The one query that ties the two data sources together.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(dest_port) AS ports_probed, dc(dest_ip) AS hosts_probed, count AS denies BY _time, src_ip
| where ports_probed >= 20 OR hosts_probed >= 25
| eval scan_time=_time
| join type=left src_ip
    [ search <4624 base search + extractions>
    | rename SrcIp AS src_ip
    | stats min(_time) AS logon_time, values(TargetUser) AS users, values(host) AS targets BY src_ip ]
| where isnotnull(logon_time) AND logon_time > scan_time AND (logon_time - scan_time) < 86400
| convert ctime(scan_time) ctime(logon_time)
```

Scanning followed by a successful authentication from the same address inside 24 hours.
This is as close to "initial access" as deny-only logs can get you.

### H8 — Firewall: the denial gap

Counterintuitive but useful. A source that generates sustained denies and then stops
either gave up or found an allowed path. The second case is invisible in deny logs,
which is precisely why the silence matters.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS denies BY _time, src_ip
| streamstats window=6 avg(denies) AS trailing_avg BY src_ip
| where denies=0 AND trailing_avg > 50
```

Treat as a hunting prompt, not an alert. Pair with H7 against the same source.

### H9 — Blocked egress from a workstation

Post-compromise, but often the first thing you actually see.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| where cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", src_ip)
| where NOT (cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", dest_ip) OR cidrmatch("172.16.0.0/12", dest_ip) OR cidrmatch("192.168.0.0/16", dest_ip))
| stats count AS attempts, dc(dest_ip) AS destinations, values(dest_port) AS ports,
        earliest(_time) AS first, latest(_time) AS last BY src_ip
| eval duration_hr=round((last-first)/3600,1)
| eval rate=round(attempts/duration_hr,1)
| where attempts >= 50 AND destinations <= 5
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
```

Few destinations, high count, sustained over time — beacon shape. A workstation
repeatedly trying to reach one external address on a blocked port is worth a look
regardless of what the rest of this finds.

### H10 — 4648 anomaly

```spl
<4648 base search + extractions>
| eval same=if(lower(SubjectUser)==lower(UsedAccount),1,0)
| where same=0
| stats count, dc(TargetServer) AS servers, values(TargetServer) AS targets BY host, SubjectUser, UsedAccount
| where servers >= 3
```

One account explicitly using another's credentials against several servers is the
classic post-access lateral pattern, and 4648 is on by default.

---

## Part 3 — The gap, stated plainly

Everything above is working around missing telemetry. Three changes would do more than
any query here:

1. **Enable 4688 with command-line auditing.** One GPO setting plus
   `ProcessCreationIncludeCmdLine_Enabled`. This is the single highest-value change
   available and costs almost nothing.
2. **Log firewall allows, at minimum on the perimeter and any segmentation boundary.**
   Deny-only logging means the successful intrusion is structurally invisible. Volume is
   the usual objection — sample or scope it rather than skipping it.
3. **PowerShell script block logging (4104).** Even partially, it recovers most of what
   attackers do after initial access.

Sysmon is the obvious fourth, but the three above are native, free, and defensible as
baseline hygiene in a way that deploying an agent sometimes is not.

Until then: authentication rarity is your detection, the firewall gives you timing
brackets, and 7045 is your execution proxy.


# Windows Logon Analysis — JSON Import, Unknown Sourcetype

## Step 0 — Discovery (run these first)

With `monitor://` ingest and no sourcetype defined, Splunk guesses. Field names depend
entirely on what produced the JSON — Winlogbeat, EvtxECmd, evtx_dump, Chainsaw, native
Event Viewer export, and the Splunk TA all use different schemas for the same event.

### 0a. What sourcetype did Splunk actually assign?

```spl
index=* earliest=-30d
| stats count, values(source) AS files BY index, sourcetype
| sort - count
```

### 0b. What do the fields look like?

```spl
index=<your_index> sourcetype=<found_above>
| head 1
| spath
| transpose 100
```

`spath` forces JSON parsing even when auto-KV did not fire. If `transpose` shows dotted
paths like `winlog.event_data.TargetUserName`, you have Winlogbeat. If you see
`Event.EventData.TargetUserName`, it is evtx_dump. If `EventCode` and `Account_Name`,
the Splunk TA schema. If `PayloadData1` through `PayloadData6`, that is EvtxECmd.

### 0c. Verify `_time` is real

**Check this before building any timeline.** If Splunk did not recognize a timestamp
field in the JSON, it falls back to file modification time or index time — which makes
every event appear to have happened when you copied the files in.

```spl
index=<your_index>
| eval indexed=_indextime
| eval drift=abs(_time - indexed)
| stats count, min(_time) AS earliest, max(_time) AS latest, avg(drift) AS avg_drift_sec
| convert ctime(earliest) ctime(latest)
```

If `earliest` and `latest` cluster within minutes of each other, or `avg_drift_sec` is
near zero across events that should span weeks, `_time` is wrong. Fix by extracting the
event's own timestamp:

```spl
| spath
| eval evt_time=coalesce('winlog.event_time', 'Event.System.TimeCreated.#attributes.SystemTime',
                         'TimeCreated', 'timestamp', '@timestamp', 'UtcTime', 'SystemTime')
| eval _time=strptime(evt_time, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%N%Z")
```

Long term, fix it properly in `props.conf` with `TIMESTAMP_FIELDS` and `TIME_FORMAT`
and reindex. The `eval` above is a search-time bandage.

---

## Step 1 — Normalization block

Paste this after every base search below. It coalesces across the common schemas so the
rest of the queries work regardless of exporter.

```spl
| spath
| eval EventID=coalesce(EventCode, EventID, 'winlog.event_id', 'Event.System.EventID', event_id, EventId)
| eval TargetUser=coalesce(Account_Name, TargetUserName, 'winlog.event_data.TargetUserName',
                           'Event.EventData.TargetUserName', target_user)
| eval TargetDomain=coalesce(TargetDomainName, 'winlog.event_data.TargetDomainName',
                             'Event.EventData.TargetDomainName')
| eval SubjectUser=coalesce(SubjectUserName, 'winlog.event_data.SubjectUserName',
                            'Event.EventData.SubjectUserName')
| eval SrcIp=coalesce(Source_Network_Address, IpAddress, 'winlog.event_data.IpAddress',
                      'Event.EventData.IpAddress', src_ip)
| eval SrcPort=coalesce(IpPort, 'winlog.event_data.IpPort', 'Event.EventData.IpPort')
| eval LogonType=coalesce(Logon_Type, LogonType, 'winlog.event_data.LogonType',
                          'Event.EventData.LogonType')
| eval Workstation=coalesce(WorkstationName, 'winlog.event_data.WorkstationName',
                            'Event.EventData.WorkstationName')
| eval LogonId=coalesce(TargetLogonId, 'winlog.event_data.TargetLogonId',
                        'Event.EventData.TargetLogonId')
| eval TargetSid=coalesce(TargetUserSid, 'winlog.event_data.TargetUserSid',
                          'Event.EventData.TargetUserSid')
| eval Privileges=coalesce(PrivilegeList, 'winlog.event_data.PrivilegeList',
                           'Event.EventData.PrivilegeList')
| eval SubStatus=coalesce(SubStatus, 'winlog.event_data.SubStatus', 'Event.EventData.SubStatus')
| eval Computer=coalesce(ComputerName, Computer, host, 'winlog.computer_name',
                         'Event.System.Computer')
| eval AuthPackage=coalesce(AuthenticationPackageName, 'winlog.event_data.AuthenticationPackageName')
| eval LogonProcess=coalesce(LogonProcessName, 'winlog.event_data.LogonProcessName')
| eval SrcIp=if(SrcIp IN ("-","::1","127.0.0.1",""), "local", SrcIp)
| eval logon_desc=case(
    LogonType=="2","Interactive", LogonType=="3","Network", LogonType=="4","Batch",
    LogonType=="5","Service", LogonType=="7","Unlock", LogonType=="8","NetworkCleartext",
    LogonType=="9","NewCredentials", LogonType=="10","RDP", LogonType=="11","CachedInteractive",
    LogonType=="12","CachedRemoteInteractive", LogonType=="13","CachedUnlock",
    1=1,"unknown")
```

Delete the coalesce branches that do not apply once you know your schema — this block is
expensive and most of it will be dead weight.

---

## Query 1 — Admin logons: who, where, when, from where

**4672 is the reliable admin signal.** It fires immediately after 4624 when the session
is granted sensitive privileges, and it shares a `LogonId` with its 4624. That beats
guessing from account names, which misses renamed admin accounts and flags service
accounts that merely have "admin" in the string.

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode=4624 OR EventCode=4672 OR EventID=4624 OR EventID=4672)
<normalization block>
| eval is_admin_event=if(EventID=="4672",1,0)
| eventstats max(is_admin_event) AS admin_session BY LogonId, Computer
| where EventID="4624" AND admin_session=1
| where NOT TargetUser IN ("SYSTEM","LOCAL SERVICE","NETWORK SERVICE","ANONYMOUS LOGON")
| where NOT match(TargetUser, "\$$")
| table _time, TargetUser, TargetDomain, Computer, logon_desc, LogonType, SrcIp, SrcPort, Workstation, LogonProcess, AuthPackage, LogonId
| sort - _time
```

The `\$$` filter drops machine accounts, which trigger 4672 constantly and are almost
never what you want.

### Secondary admin signals (use if 4672 is absent)

```spl
| eval admin_by_rid=if(match(TargetSid, "-500$") OR match(TargetSid, "-(512|519|518|516)$"),1,0)
| eval admin_by_name=if(match(lower(TargetUser), "^(da_|sa_|adm|admin)|_adm$|-a$|\.adm$"),1,0)
| eval admin_by_priv=if(match(Privileges, "(SeDebugPrivilege|SeTcbPrivilege|SeLoadDriverPrivilege|SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege|SeBackupPrivilege|SeRestorePrivilege|SeImpersonatePrivilege)"),1,0)
| eval admin_confidence=admin_by_rid+admin_by_name+admin_by_priv
| where admin_confidence >= 1
```

RID `-500` is the built-in Administrator regardless of rename. `-512` Domain Admins,
`-519` Enterprise Admins, `-518` Schema Admins, `-516` Domain Controllers.

### Summary rollup

```spl
<query 1 above, replacing the table with:>
| stats count AS logons, dc(Computer) AS machines, values(Computer) AS machine_list,
        dc(SrcIp) AS sources, values(SrcIp) AS source_list,
        values(logon_desc) AS methods,
        min(_time) AS first, max(_time) AS last
  BY TargetUser
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
| sort - machines
```

Sorting by `machines` puts the accounts touching the most systems at the top — usually
the right place to start.

