## Query 2 — Failed logins, timeline format

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode=4625 OR EventID=4625)
<normalization block>
| eval failure_reason=case(
    SubStatus=="0xC0000064","account does not exist",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006A","wrong password",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000234","account locked out",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000072","account disabled",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000070","workstation restriction",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000193","account expired",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000071","password expired",
    SubStatus=="0xC000015B","logon type not granted",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006D","bad credentials (generic)",
    SubStatus=="0xC000006E","account restriction",
    SubStatus=="0xC0000224","must change password",
    1=1, SubStatus)
| table _time, TargetUser, TargetDomain, Computer, logon_desc, SrcIp, Workstation, failure_reason, SubStatus
| sort - _time
```

**The substatus is the most important field here.** `0xC0000064` across many accounts is
username enumeration. `0xC000006A` across many accounts is password spraying. Same event
ID, different attack stage.

### Visual timeline

```spl
<4625 base + normalization>
| timechart span=15m count BY TargetUser limit=15 useother=t
```

Swap `BY TargetUser` for `BY SrcIp` or `BY Computer` depending on the question. Adjust
`span` to the window — 15m for a day, 1h for a week.

### Failure-to-success sequence (the actual finding)

Failures alone are background noise. Failures followed by a success from the same source
is the thing worth waking up for.

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode IN (4624,4625) OR EventID IN (4624,4625))
<normalization block>
| where SrcIp!="local" AND NOT match(TargetUser,"\$$")
| sort 0 SrcIp, _time
| streamstats count(eval(EventID=="4625")) AS running_fails BY SrcIp, TargetUser
| streamstats current=f last(_time) AS prev_time BY SrcIp, TargetUser
| where EventID=="4624" AND running_fails >= 5
| eval gap_sec=round(_time-prev_time,0)
| table _time, TargetUser, Computer, SrcIp, logon_desc, running_fails, gap_sec
| sort - running_fails
```

