## Query 3 — What else to look at

### 3a. Password spray shape

```spl
<4625 base + normalization>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(TargetUser) AS users_tried, count AS attempts, dc(Computer) AS targets,
        values(failure_reason) AS reasons BY _time, SrcIp
| eval per_user=round(attempts/users_tried,2)
| where users_tried >= 10 AND per_user <= 3
| sort - users_tried
```

Low attempts per user across many users is the signature — designed to stay under
lockout thresholds, which is exactly why per-account alerting misses it.

### 3b. Type 9 — NewCredentials

`runas /netonly`. Legitimate uses exist but are narrow. Heavily associated with
pass-the-hash and overpass-the-hash tooling.

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode=4624 OR EventID=4624)
<normalization block>
| where LogonType="9"
| table _time, SubjectUser, TargetUser, TargetDomain, Computer, LogonProcess, SrcIp
| sort - _time
```

`seclogo` as the LogonProcess with a mismatched subject/target pair is the classic
pattern.

### 3c. Anonymous and null-session logons

```spl
<4624 base + normalization>
| where TargetUser="ANONYMOUS LOGON" OR TargetSid="S-1-5-7"
| stats count, values(Computer) AS targets, dc(Computer) AS target_count BY SrcIp
| where SrcIp!="local"
| sort - count
```

### 3d. Explicit credential use (4648)

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode=4648 OR EventID=4648)
<normalization block>
| eval UsedAccount=coalesce(TargetUserName, 'winlog.event_data.TargetUserName')
| where lower(SubjectUser)!=lower(UsedAccount)
| stats count, dc(Computer) AS hosts, values(UsedAccount) AS creds_used,
        values(Computer) AS hosts_list BY SubjectUser
| where hosts >= 2
| sort - hosts
```

Underused and on by default. One user explicitly wielding another's credentials across
multiple hosts is the standard lateral movement shape.

### 3e. Account and group changes

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode IN (4720,4722,4724,4726,4728,4732,4756,4738,4767)
                 OR EventID IN (4720,4722,4724,4726,4728,4732,4756,4738,4767))
<normalization block>
| eval action=case(
    EventID=="4720","user created", EventID=="4722","user enabled",
    EventID=="4724","password reset by admin", EventID=="4726","user deleted",
    EventID=="4728","added to global group", EventID=="4732","added to local group",
    EventID=="4756","added to universal group", EventID=="4738","user changed",
    EventID=="4767","account unlocked")
| table _time, SubjectUser, TargetUser, action, Computer
| sort - _time
```

**4728/4732/4756 into a privileged group is the event to alert on.** Privilege
escalation shows up here before it shows up anywhere else you have visibility into.

### 3f. Lockouts (4740)

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode=4740 OR EventID=4740)
<normalization block>
| stats count, values(Workstation) AS source_workstations BY TargetUser
| sort - count
```

Often just a stale mapped drive or phone email client. Sometimes the tail end of a
spray. Worth separating.

### 3g. Log cleared (1102 / 104)

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode IN (1102,104) OR EventID IN (1102,104))
<normalization block>
| table _time, Computer, SubjectUser, EventID
| sort - _time
```

Rare, and almost never benign outside a documented maintenance window. Check this even
if nothing else fires.

### 3h. Session duration via logoff pairing

```spl
index=<your_index> (EventCode IN (4624,4634,4647) OR EventID IN (4624,4634,4647))
<normalization block>
| transaction LogonId startswith="4624" endswith=eval(EventID IN ("4634","4647")) maxspan=24h
| where LogonType IN ("2","10")
| eval duration_min=round(duration/60,1)
| table _time, TargetUser, Computer, logon_desc, SrcIp, duration_min
| sort - duration_min
```

`transaction` is expensive. For large volumes use `stats min(_time) max(_time) BY LogonId`
instead.

### 3i. Rare pairings (highest value per unit effort)

```spl
<4624 base + normalization>
| where LogonType IN ("3","10") AND SrcIp!="local"
| stats min(_time) AS first_seen, count BY TargetUser, Computer, SrcIp
| eventstats min(first_seen) AS user_first_ever BY TargetUser
| where first_seen > relative_time(now(),"-7d") AND first_seen!=user_first_ever
| convert ctime(first_seen)
| sort first_seen
```

An account reaching a machine it has never reached before. Rarity beats thresholds for
intrusion detection, and this one query surfaces more real findings than most signature
work.

